# Listen-Fire self-host — copy to `deploy/.env` and fill in.
#
# This file is YOURS: the things only you know. Everything machine-generated
# (database password, token secret, both encryption keys, the team id, the API
# key) is minted on first boot into the `listen-fire-config` docker volume and must
# never be edited or regenerated. Back that volume up.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ model ---
# Any ONE of these is what gives this installation agents, extraction and
# arbitration of conflicting facts. Anthropic is the default provider for the
# agents. Without one the stack starts and serves — the graph, the CRM writes,
# the query surfaces and the whole of core work — and those three fail at the
# moment they are asked for, naming the key. `init` and the API both warn at
# every boot. Leave it empty for a demo, where there is nothing to call.
# Each key serves the model names MODEL_MAP leaves at that vendor — see
# "model map" below for sending names elsewhere.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# KNOWLEDGE_LLM_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Optional. Sent as the OpenAI client's `organization` on OpenAI's own API.
# Leave unset to use the key's own default organisation — the right choice
# for a key that was never minted inside a particular org.
# OPENAI_ORGANIZATION=
# Off by default, trusting the model. `1` (or `true`) puts an extraction's
# reply on a tight, input-sized output leash instead of the normal flat
# ceiling — only worth setting for a provider/model that is known to loop.
# EXTRACTION_OUTPUT_BUDGET=
# -------------------------------------------------------------- model map ---
# Which vendor answers each model name the product uses. Unset or empty, every
# name goes to its own vendor with the keys above. Otherwise a JSON object on
# one line, from model name to `provider/wire-model`, where the provider is
# anthropic, vertex (Claude on Google Cloud), openai or gemini (Gemini on
# Google Cloud) and the wire model is that vendor's own spelling. Boot refuses a
# map it cannot serve, naming the line. deploy/SELF_HOSTING.md has three worked
# maps (vendor keys, everything on OpenAI, everything on Gemini) and one for
# Claude through Google Cloud; a short one:
# MODEL_MAP={"claude-sonnet-5":"vertex/claude-sonnet-5","whisper-1":"gemini/gemini-3.8-flash"}
# Before sending Claude to vertex, in the Google project: enable the Agent
# Platform API (`aiplatform.googleapis.com`), and switch on each Claude model
# you use in Model Garden.
# What each extraction tier buys: a model name (routed by MODEL_MAP like any
# other) and an effort (low, medium, high, xhigh). Unset, every tier is
# claude-sonnet-5, at low, high and xhigh. A tier or field left out keeps that
# default; boot refuses a model this deployment cannot call.
# EXTRACTION_TIERS={"careful":{"model":"claude-opus-5","effort":"medium"}}
# Where Claude is addressed on Vertex. `global` is the default and the only
# region without a price premium; it need not match GOOGLE_PROJECT_LOCATION
# below, which is a real region OCR and image generation use instead.
# GOOGLE_MODEL_REGION=global

# ----------------------------------------------------------- google cloud ---
# The one Google service account this deployment authenticates with — for
# MODEL_MAP lines naming vertex or gemini above, and, on their own, for Google
# Document AI (OCR).
# All required together for either feature; GOOGLE_PROJECT_LOCATION defaults
# to europe-west1 if left unset.
# GOOGLE_PRIVATE_KEY=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_EMAIL=
# GOOGLE_PROJECT_ID=
# GOOGLE_PROJECT_LOCATION=
# OCR-only, on top of the four above: without every one of these, OCR boots
# unconfigured and a PDF past its text layer fails naming what it wanted.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OCR_PROCESSOR_ID=
# GOOGLE_STORAGE_BUCKET_NAME=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ gmail ---
# The OAuth client a Gmail sign-in runs through. Optional, and BOTH or NEITHER:
# half a pair fails boot, because Google refuses an exchange carrying an id
# without its secret. Set them to put Gmail on a client you registered for it —
# your own consent screen, your own verification, your own refresh tokens.
# Unset, Gmail rides GOOGLE_INTEGRATIONS_CLIENT_ID / _SECRET below, the client
# Sheets and Drive already use.
# GMAIL_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GMAIL_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# How a mailbox is connected. `oauth` (the default) signs in AS the mailbox
# through the client above, and Google confines the resulting token to that one
# address; a Workspace admin who would rather authorise the mailbox themselves
# pastes the resulting refresh token on the same connect form, where it is
# redeemed with that same client. `delegated` instead has the service account
# above act as an address it never signed into, which a Workspace admin grants
# once. Anything else refuses to boot.
# GMAIL_CONNECT_METHOD=oauth
#
# Whether a connected mailbox may SEND. Off unless set to exactly `true`: the
# scope is only asked for when the installation wants it, so a read-only
# deployment never shows a user a consent screen asking to send their mail.
# Turning it on takes effect on the next connect — an already-connected mailbox
# is reconnected to gain the scope.
# GMAIL_SEND_ENABLED=true
#
# Which mailboxes this deployment may act as. Comma separated, case
# insensitive. Set, it is enforced under BOTH methods. UNSET, the two differ:
#   oauth     — no restriction. A sign-in's token works for the one mailbox
#               that signed in and no other, so Google already draws the line.
#   delegated — no mailbox may be connected or used, on purpose. Domain wide
#               delegation has no per-mailbox limit of its own, so defaulting
#               to "every mailbox" would be a guarantee that silently is not
#               one. This list is REQUIRED under that method.
# GMAIL_MAILBOX_ALLOWLIST=ops@example.com,deals@example.com

# --------------------------------------------------------------- version ---
# WHICH release this installation runs. `up.sh` and `docker compose` pull
# `ghcr.io/listen-fire/<name>:<this>`; unset means `latest`, which moves under
# you the next time anything pulls. PINNING A TAG HERE IS HOW AN INSTALLATION
# CHOOSES WHEN IT MOVES: it stays on that release until you edit this line and
# restart. See UPGRADING.md for what an upgrade involves.
# LISTEN_FIRE_VERSION=v0.1.0

# ------------------------------------------------------------- safeguards ---
# The most network calls one automation run may make to Affinity before it is
# stopped and fails, naming the limit. Default 2000 — high enough that a large
# but honest run never notices, low enough that a read which forgot to narrow
# itself stops before it eats the API quota.
# AFFINITY_MAX_CALLS_PER_RUN=2000
# The most one automation run may spend on model calls, in US dollars. Once a
# run has spent it, its next model call is refused and the run fails, naming
# the cap and what it spent. Unset = no cap. Not mandatory, but encouraged: a
# looping automation keeps making honest model calls and nothing else stops it.
# Boot refuses a value that is not a positive number.
# MOVEMENT_MAX_RUN_COST_USD=5
# How many calls deep one automation run may nest — a function calling itself,
# directly or through others, counts one per level. Past it the run fails,
# naming the chain of calls. Default 32. Boot refuses a value that is not a
# positive whole number. See deploy/SELF_HOSTING.md ("Automations").
# MOVEMENT_MAX_CALL_DEPTH=32
# How much of ONE attached document's text an extraction may read. Default
# 50000 characters — a twenty-page deck whole. Past it the prompt carries the
# first N characters and says so, to the model and on the run, rather than
# letting one lookbook crowd out every other source on every staged call.
# EXTRACTION_FILE_CHARS=50000
# The largest PDF whose own text layer is read before the OCR provider is
# asked. Default 67108864 bytes (64 MB); a larger file streams straight to OCR.
# MAX_PDF_BUFFER_BYTES=67108864

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- naming ---
LISTEN_FIRE_TEAM_NAME=Acme
LISTEN_FIRE_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# The name your Slack app shows in a workspace — you register that app, so the
# name belongs to you. Defaults to `Listen-Fire`.
# SLACK_APP_DISPLAY_NAME=
# The app name Meta substitutes into your WhatsApp authentication template —
# the template is registered per deployment. Defaults to `Listen-Fire`.
# WHATSAPP_APP_DISPLAY_NAME=
# How a user proves a WhatsApp number is theirs. `otp` (the default) sends a
# code through that template. `trust` links a signed-in user's number at once,
# with no code: anyone with an account can then claim any number, so only for
# a deployment serving one organisation. Anything else fails boot.
# WHATSAPP_LINK_VERIFICATION=otp
# Where a user with a problem is told to go. No default: unset means this
# installation publishes no address rather than inventing one.
# SUPPORT_EMAIL=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- ports ---
# HOST ports. The containers always listen on their own fixed ports. Port
# NUMBERS only — the address they bind to is LISTEN_FIRE_BIND, below.
#
# THIS FILE WINS, including under `up.sh`: it supplies these (and API_BASE_URL /
# WEB_BASE_URL below) only as defaults for an installation that names none.
WEB_PORT=8080
API_PORT=8081
ADMIN_PORT=8082
FAKE_CHANNELS_PORT_HOST=8083
# WHICH host address those ports answer on. `127.0.0.1` is what a deployment
# behind a reverse proxy on the same box wants: the proxy reaches the stack and
# nothing else can, so the firewall stops being the only thing between the
# internet and a plain-HTTP port. Unset means every interface, which is what a
# trial on your own machine wants.
# LISTEN_FIRE_BIND=127.0.0.1

# ----------------------------------------------------------- composition ---
# WHICH units run, and which identity provider validates credentials. `up.sh`
# derives both from the units you name on its command line and exports them, so
# leave them commented for a trial. Set them here when you run `docker compose`
# yourself — which is what a deployment behind a real hostname does. Unset means
# every unit, with core identity. The process checks the pair against each other
# at boot in both directions: `core` in the product list and `core` identity
# imply each other, and either half without the other refuses to start.
# LISTEN_FIRE_PRODUCTS=knowledge,automations
# LISTEN_FIRE_PRINCIPAL=static

# ------------------------------------------------------------ datastores ---
# WHICH optional services this composition runs. `up.sh` names these on the
# command line itself; this line is what a bare `docker compose` reads. Leave it
# alone: the three datastore profiles below turn themselves OFF when you name a
# datastore of your own, and `admin` (the operator console) and `demo` (the
# stand-in third parties) are the two you might add by hand. (`minio`, the
# object store's profile name before SeaweedFS replaced MinIO, still works.)
COMPOSE_PROFILES=postgres,redis,objectstore
#
# Postgres, Redis and the object store are bundled, and each can be moved to a
# managed service on its own by naming it here — nothing else changes, and the
# bundled service stops being part of the composition. See SELF_HOSTING.md,
# "Bringing your own datastores", and your platform's guide for the runbook that
# moves the data.
#
# Your own database. Set DATABASE_URL_READONLY too only if you have a replica;
# left unset it follows DATABASE_URL, which is what it must be either way.
# Run deploy/postgres-init/00-roles.sql against it, as a superuser, FIRST.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/listenfire
# DATABASE_URL_READONLY=
# Your own Redis (optional: the API connects to one only when the hostname is set; without it the
# loop-guard counters stay per process and the icon cache is off). Host and port, no URL form: the client is built from these two
# and nothing else, so a Redis that demands AUTH or TLS cannot be reached.
# MESSAGE_QUEUE_REDIS_HOSTNAME=
# MESSAGE_QUEUE_REDIS_PORT=6379
# Your own object store — see "file storage" below, which is the same switch.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- graph mode ---
# `webhook` delivers graph mutations to the subscriptions you register; `local`
# hands them to an in-process consumer that only exists when `automations` is
# mounted in this same process. `up.sh` sets this for you from the units you
# name — override only if you know why.
# KNOWLEDGE_MUTATION_DELIVERY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ urls ---
# Where this installation is reachable from the internet. Unset, `up.sh`
# defaults both to localhost for a trial; set them here for anything behind a
# real hostname and they are used as written, `up.sh` included.
# They also decide cookie security: the session cookie is `Secure` only when
# these are https, and a `Secure` cookie on plain http is silently dropped.
# API_BASE_URL=
# WEB_BASE_URL=
# Where an OAuth consent screen returns the user. Those callback paths are
# pages in the WEB app, so this is the WEB origin — and compose defaults it to
# WEB_BASE_URL. Set it only if the web app answers somewhere else.
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE_URL=
# The origin a third party fetches an exposed file's bytes from — the API's,
# which is what compose defaults it to. (The code's own fallback is
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE_URL and then a localhost literal, so off compose set it.)
# EXPOSED_FILE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------ integrations ---
# Every one of these is optional. Absent means the corresponding connector is
# simply not offered; nothing else changes. See SELF_HOSTING.md, "Registering
# your own third-party apps", for what to register and what each one costs.
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_CLIENT_ID=
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_SIGNING_SECRET=
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_STATE_SECRET=
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_REDIRECT_URI=
# Attio: without this pair, Attio connects with a pasted workspace access token.
# ATTIO_CLIENT_ID=
# ATTIO_CLIENT_SECRET=
# AIRTABLE_CLIENT_ID=
# AIRTABLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Google: one client for Sheets, Drive AND Gmail — same project, different
# scopes. Its redirect URIs need /google-integrations/callback and, for Gmail,
# /gmail/callback. (Gmail uses GMAIL_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID instead when that is set.)
# GOOGLE_INTEGRATIONS_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_INTEGRATIONS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# DROPBOX_CLIENT_ID=
# DROPBOX_CLIENT_SECRET=
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Email: pick ONE provider. Resend wins if both are set, inbound and out.
# MAILGUN_API_KEY=
# MAILGUN_SENDING_DOMAIN=
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# OUTBOUND_EMAIL_FROM=
# The address automations receive mail on — local part and domain. No default:
# one would tell your authors to forward their mail to somebody else's inbox.
# INBOUND_EMAIL_ADDRESS=inbox@example.com
# SENTRY_DSN=

# ---------------------------------------------------- duplicate matching ---
# Jev (Typesafe AI) is an optional non-generative judge for FUZZY duplicate
# matching: probabilities over the candidate records instead of a free text
# reply, about ten times faster than the model judge. JEV_ENTITY_RESOLUTION=true
# switches the judge to Jev and fails boot without JEV_KEY; a key alone
# changes nothing.
# JEV_KEY=
# JEV_ENTITY_RESOLUTION=

# ------------------------------------------------------------- web search ---
# WEB_SEARCH_PROVIDER picks which of the two below actually runs a search:
# `google` (the default when unset) or `brightdata`. Anything else fails boot
# naming the variable and the two values. The plugins that go looking for
# something on the open web — the LinkedIn activity research and the website
# research — search through whichever is selected; without it configured,
# those two plugins cannot run and every record they were pointed at comes
# back unenriched.
# WEB_SEARCH_PROVIDER=google

# `google`: a Google Custom Search key and the search-engine id it belongs to.
# Google is discontinuing "search the entire web" in Programmable Search on
# 2027-01-01 — plan to move to `brightdata` before then.
# GOOGLE_CUSTOM_SEARCH_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_CX=

# `brightdata`: the SERP API, returning Google's own results as JSON. Shares
# the BRIGHT_DATA_ACCESS_TOKEN a Bright Data page-scraping setup already
# has, but needs its own zone — a SERP zone is a separate product from the
# Web Unlocker zone the page scraper uses.
# BRIGHT_DATA_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# BRIGHT_DATA_SERP_ZONE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------- file storage ---
# Features that handle a file need an S3-compatible bucket; there is no
# local-filesystem driver. Leave every line here unset and this installation's
# own SeaweedFS (the `objectstore` service) is that bucket, on a docker volume,
# wired up for you. Upgrading an installation that ran the bundled MinIO? Read
# SELF_HOSTING.md, "Upgrading from the bundled MinIO", first.
#
# SETTING AWS_S3_ENDPOINT is what says the store is yours: the bundled store
# then leaves the composition, and these four are yours to fill in — all four
# together, or no storage at all, and a missing one means file-touching steps
# fail naming what they wanted.
# AWS_S3_ENDPOINT=https://storage.googleapis.com
# AWS_DOCUMENT_S3_BUCKET=
# AWS_REGION=auto
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# MinIO, SeaweedFS and Ceph need path-style addressing. Only the literal lowercase `true`
# turns it on: `TRUE` and `1` read as false.
# AWS_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=

# Telegram, WhatsApp, Twilio, the narrowing settings for an API key
# (LISTEN_FIRE_SCOPES, LISTEN_FIRE_ACCESS) and the rest are in SELF_HOSTING.md's
# environment reference, with what each one does when unset.
